<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-12" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Calculus of Vector-Valued Functions</title>
   <introduction>
    <p>
      Since limits are calculated component-wise for vector-valued functions, derivatives and integrals are also calculated component-wise
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <objectives>
    <title>Calculus of Vector-Valued Functions </title>
     <introduction>

    <p>
    In this section we will look at:
    </p>

  </introduction>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Derivatives of vector-valued functions
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Integrals of vector-valued functions
        </p>
      </li>
          </ul>
  </objectives>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-vvf-derivatives">
    <title>Derivatives of Vector-Valued Functions</title>
    <p>
      To calculate the derivative of a vector-valued function, calculate the derivative of each component.
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Consider the function: <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \langle te^{-t}, \arctan(t), 2e^{3t} \rangle</m>, then
        <md>
          \mathbf{r}'(t) = \left\langle e^{-t}-te^{-t}, \frac{1}{1+t^2}, 6e^{3t}\right\rangle
        </md>
        </p>
    </example>
    <p>
    We can also calculate with combinations of scalar functions and vector-valued functions. Here are the relevant rules.
    </p>
    <assemblage>
      <title>Vector Differentiation Rules</title>
      <p>
       <md>
        <mrow>\text{Linearity Rules} \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, \amp \frac{d}{dt}[c\cdot \mathbf{u}(t)] = c\cdot\mathbf{u}'(t)  </mrow>
       <mrow>\text{and}\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, \amp\frac{d}{dt}[\mathbf{u}(t)+\mathbf{v}(t)] = \mathbf{u}'(t) + \mathbf{v}'(t) </mrow>
        <mrow>\text{Product Rule}\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, \amp \frac{d}{dt}[f(t)\cdot\mathbf{u}(t)]=f'(t)\cdot\mathbf{u}(t) + f(t)\cdot\mathbf{u}'(t)</mrow>
       <mrow>\text{Dot Product Rule} \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\amp \frac{d}{dt}[\mathbf{u}(t)\cdot\mathbf{v}(t)]=\mathbf{u}'(t)\cdot\mathbf{v}(t) + \mathbf{u}(t)\cdot\mathbf{v}'(t)</mrow>
       <mrow>\text{Cross Product Rule}\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, \amp \frac{d}{dt}[\mathbf{u}(t)\times\mathbf{v}(t)]=\mathbf{u}'(t)\times\mathbf{v}(t) + \mathbf{u}(t)\times\mathbf{v}'(t)</mrow>
       <mrow>\text{Chain Rule} \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\amp \frac{d}{dt}[\mathbf{u}(f(t))]=\mathbf{u}'(f(t))f'(t)</mrow>
      </md>
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
      Recall that be definition, the derivative is by definition te slope of a line tangent to a function at a given point. For vector-valued function, we can also create 
      a tangent line but more often we are interested in creating a tangent vector.
    </p>
    <example>
      <title>Tangent Line to a Space Curve</title>
      <p>
        Consider the space curve: <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \langle 2\cos(t), \sin(t),t \rangle </m> at the point <m>t=\frac{\pi}{2}</m>. 
        <md>
          \mathbf{r}'(t) = \langle -2\sin(t), \cos(t),1 \rangle 
        </md>
        plugging in the value <m>t=\frac{\pi}{2}</m> gives the vector <m>\mathbf{r}'(\pi/2)=\langle -2, 0 ,1</m> which is a vector of slopes. A point on the line is
        <m>\mathbf{r}(\pi/2) = \langle 0, 1, \frac{\pi}{2}\rangle </m>. The "point-slope" form of the tangent line would be <m>\mathbf{y}(t) = \langle -2, 0 ,1 \rangle (t-\frac{\pi}{2})
          +\langle 0, 1, \frac{\pi}{2}\rangle = \langle -2t, 1, t + \frac{\pi}{2} \rangle </m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Try plotting <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m> and <m>\mathbf{y}(t)</m> together to see the tangent line and space curve!
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      For many applications, like those we will cover in the next section, it is more important to calculate a tangent vector and unit tangent vector. The unit tangent vector is
      <md>
        \mathbf{T}(t) = \frac{\mathbf{r}'(t)}{||\mathbf{r}'(t)||}
      </md>
      is the principal unit vector. The unit tangent vector can be calculated for both two-dimensional and three-dimensional vectors.
    </p>
    <example>
      <title>Unit Tangent Vector</title>
      <p>
        Consider the function: <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \langle \cos(t), \sin(t), t \rangle</m>. Then
        <md>
          \mathbf{r}'(t) = \langle -\sin(t), \cos(t), 1 \rangle \,\,\,\text{and}
        </md>
        <md>
          ||\mathbf{r}'(t)|| = \sqrt{\sin^2(t)+\cos^2(t) + 1}=\sqrt{1+1} = \sqrt{2} \,\,\, \text{so}'
        </md>
        <md>
          \mathbf{T}(t) = \frac{\langle -\sin(t), \cos(t), 1 \rangle}{\sqrt{2}}
        </md>
        More on this in the next section.
      </p>
      
    </example>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-vvf-integrals">
    <title>Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions</title>
    <p>
    Both integrals and antiderivatives can be calculated component-wise for vector-valued functions.
    </p>
    <example>
      <title>An Antiderivative</title>
      <p>
        <md>
          \int \langle \sec^2(t), t(t^2+1)^3, t^2\ln(t) \rangle dt = \left\langle \tan(t), \frac{1}{8}(t^2+1)^4, \frac{1}{3}t^3\ln(t)-\frac{1}{9}t^3 \right\rangle + C
        </md>
        The second antiderivative is by the substitution <m>s=t^1+1</m> and the third antiderivative was found using integration by parts.
      </p>
      
    </example>
    <example>
      <title>A Definite Integral</title>
      <p>
        <md>
          \int_1^2 \langle t^2, t\sqrt{t-1}, t\sin(\pi t) \rangle dt = \left. \left\langle \frac{1}{3}t^3, -\frac{1}{15}(3t+4)(t-1)^{\frac{3}{2}}, \frac{1}{\pi^2}\sin(\pi t)-\frac{t}{\pi}\cos(\pi t) \right\rangle \right\vert_1^2\\
          = \left\langle \frac{8}{3}, \frac{2}{3},-\frac{2}{\pi} \right\rangle - \left\langle \frac{1}{3}, 0, \frac{1}{\pi} \right\rangle = \left\langle \frac{7}{3}, \frac{2}{3}, -\frac{3}{\pi} \right\rangle
        </md>
          </p>
      
      
    </example>
    <paragraphs>
    <title>Reading Check-in</title>
    <p>
      Check our WAMAP course page for a few questions that go along with today's reading on the calculus of vector-valued functions.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>
  </subsection>
</section>
